  "reason": "The records establish several relevant general principles: rapid cooling or processing can retain multiphase HEA microstructures; composition and thermal history can tune phase fractions; Ti- or Cr-containing HEAs can form complex or Laves phases; and multiphase decomposition or antiferromagnetic-element substitution can reduce saturation magnetization while increasing coercivity. R1 is the closest magnetic precedent, while R3, R5, R16, R17, and R19 provide related phase-engineering precedents. However, none identifies equiatomic FeCoNiTi, the specific fcc–C14 segregation, a compositionally matched C14-dominant control, or the combined sublattice-antiferromagnetism, orbital/spin-canting, and domain-wall-pinning mechanism. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but reconstructing the specific intervention and mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
